1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,700
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,720 --> 00:00:08,420
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:08,820 --> 00:00:14,591
This is Lecture 20, lockstitch and overlock control prototypes.

4
00:00:14,991 --> 00:00:18,473
Last time we built a platform for prototypes.

5
00:00:18,493 --> 00:00:23,453
Today we use it on a real machine head. How to wire the encoder,

6
00:00:23,473 --> 00:00:28,355
how to write the trim event table, how to find top dead centre,

7
00:00:28,375 --> 00:00:33,490
how to run acceptance, and finally what changes for an overlocker.

8
00:00:33,890 --> 00:00:39,944
The mechanical retrofit, power budget, E-stop, firmware state machine,

9
00:00:39,964 --> 00:00:41,158
auto backtack,

10
00:00:41,178 --> 00:00:47,752
solenoid calibration and stopping at the fabric edge are left to the slides.

11
00:00:49,272 --> 00:00:55,423
The equipment department will turn a mechanical lockstitch head with a

12
00:00:55,443 --> 00:00:58,332
trimmer into a control prototype.

13
00:00:58,732 --> 00:01:02,002
Trimming runs at 300 r/min,

14
00:01:02,022 --> 00:01:09,312
and the moving knife must enter between 300 and 330 degrees.

15
00:01:09,712 --> 00:01:16,529
The trim solenoid pulls in in 6.2 to 9.4 milliseconds,

16
00:01:16,549 --> 00:01:22,732
depending on supply voltage and coil temperature.

17
00:01:23,132 --> 00:01:28,632
At what angle should it be energised?

18
00:01:29,032 --> 00:01:36,252
And the shop would like to trim at 700 r/min.

19
00:01:36,652 --> 00:01:38,152
Is that acceptable?

20
00:01:38,552 --> 00:01:40,136
We'll answer at the end.

21
00:01:41,656 --> 00:01:44,636
Build-up: get the hardware right first.

22
00:01:46,156 --> 00:01:47,926
Here's the build-up.

23
00:01:48,326 --> 00:01:53,082
The servo motor carries a 2500-line encoder:

24
00:01:53,102 --> 00:01:59,702
ten thousand counts per revolution after times-four decoding,

25
00:01:59,722 --> 00:02:01,765
0.036 degrees each.

26
00:02:02,165 --> 00:02:08,322
The encoder plugs into the drive, which needs it for vector control,

27
00:02:08,342 --> 00:02:14,044
and the drive forwards A, B and Z one-to-one to the controller,

28
00:02:14,064 --> 00:02:16,225
whose timer counts them.

29
00:02:16,625 --> 00:02:19,435
So the angle lives in the controller.

30
00:02:19,835 --> 00:02:23,446
Why not let the drive report the angle over the bus?

31
00:02:23,846 --> 00:02:29,906
Each frame can carry hundreds of microseconds of random delay.

32
00:02:30,306 --> 00:02:35,917
At 300 r/min, half a millisecond is 0.9 degrees,

33
00:02:35,937 --> 00:02:40,726
added straight to the knife-entry spread.

34
00:02:42,246 --> 00:02:44,866
Firmware: acting by angle.

35
00:02:46,386 --> 00:02:50,399
This is the trim event table from Lab 20-1.

36
00:02:50,799 --> 00:02:55,676
Orange is the trim command, teal the tension release,

37
00:02:55,696 --> 00:02:58,818
pale green the Chapter 14 windows,

38
00:02:58,838 --> 00:03:06,211
and the dark bars are the actual action ranges computed from the solenoid delay.

39
00:03:06,611 --> 00:03:11,210
Watch the speed switch between 300 and 700:

40
00:03:11,230 --> 00:03:15,507
the dark bars slide later and get wider.

41
00:03:15,907 --> 00:03:20,487
The faster the spindle, the more degrees the same milliseconds cover.

42
00:03:20,887 --> 00:03:25,340
So the table is written in angle, and when the trimming speed changes,

43
00:03:25,360 --> 00:03:26,747
the table must change.

44
00:03:27,147 --> 00:03:34,447
Wipe and foot lift come after the spindle stops, timed in milliseconds.

45
00:03:35,967 --> 00:03:38,521
Now the initial value.

46
00:03:38,921 --> 00:03:45,222
The actual knife entry equals the command angle plus six times n times t.

47
00:03:45,623 --> 00:03:52,411
At 300 r/min the spindle turns 1.8 degrees per millisecond;

48
00:03:52,431 --> 00:03:59,220
pull-in of 6.2 to 9.4 milliseconds is 11.2 to 17.0 degrees,

49
00:03:59,240 --> 00:04:02,683
with a midpoint of about 14.1.

50
00:04:03,082 --> 00:04:12,102
Take that from the window centre, 315, and you get 301; we use 300.

51
00:04:12,503 --> 00:04:18,751
The knife then enters between 311 and 317 degrees,

52
00:04:18,771 --> 00:04:25,396
more than ten degrees inside the window on each side.

53
00:04:26,916 --> 00:04:29,757
Bring-up: one new thing at a time.

54
00:04:31,277 --> 00:04:37,160
The encoder's Z mark has nothing to do with needle-bar top dead centre,

55
00:04:37,180 --> 00:04:40,486
and the count direction may be reversed.

56
00:04:40,886 --> 00:04:47,186
Check direction first: turn the handwheel forward, and the reading must rise.

57
00:04:47,586 --> 00:04:52,246
If it falls, invert it in the parameters; reversed,

58
00:04:52,266 --> 00:04:58,670
the velocity loop becomes positive feedback and the machine runs away.

59
00:04:59,070 --> 00:05:02,804
Then find TDC.

60
00:05:03,204 --> 00:05:10,043
The obvious idea is to find the highest point with a dial gauge.

61
00:05:10,443 --> 00:05:15,578
But watch the animation: near TDC the slider-crank hardly moves.

62
00:05:15,978 --> 00:05:23,128
1.8 degrees from TDC the needle bar has dropped only 0.01 millimetres,

63
00:05:23,148 --> 00:05:26,201
below what the gauge can read.

64
00:05:27,721 --> 00:05:32,257
The trick is to measure where the needle bar moves fast.

65
00:05:32,657 --> 00:05:38,019
The displacement curve is symmetric about the dead centres.

66
00:05:38,419 --> 00:05:43,218
Pick the reading 12 millimetres below TDC;

67
00:05:43,238 --> 00:05:49,759
note r1 as the bar passes it going down and r2 coming up.

68
00:05:50,159 --> 00:05:57,446
Their average is bottom dead centre, and the offset is 180 degrees minus that.

69
00:05:57,846 --> 00:06:04,945
Here 0.01 millimetre on the gauge is only about 0.04 degrees,

70
00:06:04,965 --> 00:06:10,546
dozens of times better than hunting for the top.

71
00:06:12,066 --> 00:06:13,258
A worked example.

72
00:06:13,658 --> 00:06:23,331
Going down the encoder reads 307.6 degrees, coming up 137.2.

73
00:06:23,731 --> 00:06:27,592
The reading wrapped through 360 in between,

74
00:06:27,612 --> 00:06:32,646
so add 360 to the second before averaging: 42.4 degrees,

75
00:06:32,666 --> 00:06:36,798
the bottom dead centre in the current reading.

76
00:06:37,198 --> 00:06:45,098
The offset is 180 minus 42.4, which is 137.6 degrees.

77
00:06:45,498 --> 00:06:51,996
Write that into the parameters, and the reading follows the book's convention:

78
00:06:52,016 --> 00:06:54,921
zero is needle-bar top dead centre.

79
00:06:56,441 --> 00:06:59,763
Acceptance: running is not meeting spec.

80
00:07:01,283 --> 00:07:07,304
Acceptance runs automatically on the test bench: a script sends commands,

81
00:07:07,324 --> 00:07:13,263
reads back measurements, judges by requirement ID and writes the report.

82
00:07:13,663 --> 00:07:18,004
On the left, one trim: a few fast stitches,

83
00:07:18,024 --> 00:07:23,987
then the trim stitch in slow motion, with knife entry, cut,

84
00:07:24,007 --> 00:07:27,537
release and stop judged one by one.

85
00:07:27,937 --> 00:07:33,664
On the right, a hundred trims, each with random supply voltage,

86
00:07:33,684 --> 00:07:40,597
coil temperature, speed and friction; look at the pass count and the spread.

87
00:07:40,997 --> 00:07:46,853
Before the bench exists, the same script can run in software.

88
00:07:48,373 --> 00:07:51,558
Run in software under different conditions,

89
00:07:51,578 --> 00:07:56,553
the most interesting case is a calibration residual of 0.7 degrees.

90
00:07:56,953 --> 00:08:02,547
Every trim succeeds, yet every stop falls outside plus or minus one degree.

91
00:08:02,947 --> 00:08:09,999
The trim window has over ten degrees of margin, so 0.7 doesn't matter there;

92
00:08:10,019 --> 00:08:16,793
the stop tolerance is one degree, and the residual adds straight onto it.

93
00:08:17,193 --> 00:08:24,745
That's why stop error and trim success must be two separate requirements,

94
00:08:24,765 --> 00:08:26,613
judged separately.

95
00:08:27,013 --> 00:08:29,746
And what the model can't give,

96
00:08:29,766 --> 00:08:35,160
such as backtack alignment or a four-hour temperature rise,

97
00:08:35,180 --> 00:08:37,638
is marked 'to be measured'.

98
00:08:38,038 --> 00:08:38,978
No made-up numbers.

99
00:08:40,498 --> 00:08:43,442
The overlock prototype: what changes.

100
00:08:44,962 --> 00:08:49,142
Most of the overlock prototype carries over.

101
00:08:49,542 --> 00:08:52,600
The first difference is speed:

102
00:08:52,620 --> 00:08:57,832
the example machine runs at 7000 stitches a minute,

103
00:08:57,852 --> 00:09:02,551
but the platform motor tops out at 6000 r/min,

104
00:09:02,571 --> 00:09:05,936
so we gear up with a timing belt.

105
00:09:06,336 --> 00:09:09,516
Here's an easily missed point.

106
00:09:09,916 --> 00:09:16,114
At a ratio of 1.4, one spindle revolution is 7142.857 counts,

107
00:09:16,134 --> 00:09:18,051
not a whole number,

108
00:09:18,071 --> 00:09:25,899
so the Z mark lands at a different spindle angle every turn and can't correct

109
00:09:25,919 --> 00:09:26,817
anything,

110
00:09:26,837 --> 00:09:29,773
and rounding error builds up.

111
00:09:30,173 --> 00:09:35,742
At 1.25, 30 teeth to 24, it's exactly 8000.

112
00:09:36,142 --> 00:09:39,300
Even then a belt can skip a tooth,

113
00:09:39,320 --> 00:09:43,600
so a needle-up sensor checks every revolution.

114
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:52,347
And a count: 11 of the overlocker's 19 items are the same as the lockstitch's;

115
00:09:52,367 --> 00:10:00,500
control, drive, power, networking and the firmware framework all carry over.

116
00:10:02,020 --> 00:10:08,880
A newly assembled robot joint is brought up just like today's prototype.

117
00:10:09,280 --> 00:10:15,939
Control power only first, checking that the E-stop really removes drive enable.

118
00:10:16,339 --> 00:10:21,662
Then a slow jog to confirm encoder direction; reversed,

119
00:10:21,682 --> 00:10:28,559
the velocity loop turns into positive feedback and the joint runs away.

120
00:10:28,959 --> 00:10:32,436
Joint zeroing also averages from both sides:

121
00:10:32,456 --> 00:10:38,556
approach the home switch slowly from the positive and the negative direction,

122
00:10:38,576 --> 00:10:44,040
and average the two trigger readings to cancel the switch hysteresis.

123
00:10:44,440 --> 00:10:48,900
And when a gripper valve picks parts off a conveyor,

124
00:10:48,920 --> 00:10:55,620
its delay is calibrated as a distance at belt speed, not just in milliseconds.

125
00:10:57,139 --> 00:11:01,239
The two-point idea works in everyday life too.

126
00:11:01,639 --> 00:11:04,642
To hang a hook in the middle of a door frame,

127
00:11:04,662 --> 00:11:07,128
eyeballing the middle is usually off.

128
00:11:07,528 --> 00:11:12,453
Measure the same distance in from each side, mark both,

129
00:11:12,473 --> 00:11:17,308
and take the midpoint of the marks: far more accurate.

130
00:11:17,708 --> 00:11:21,488
Calibrating the needle bar works the same way.

131
00:11:21,888 --> 00:11:25,701
Take the same reading on the two steep sides and average,

132
00:11:25,721 --> 00:11:29,670
instead of hunting for the top where the curve is flattest.

133
00:11:31,190 --> 00:11:32,793
Back to our problem.

134
00:11:33,193 --> 00:11:43,118
At 300 r/min pull-in is 11.2 to 17.0 degrees.

135
00:11:43,518 --> 00:11:53,938
Firing the trim solenoid at 300 puts knife entry at 311 to 317.

136
00:11:54,338 --> 00:12:03,651
The release pulls in at most about 17 degrees late, so it must fire by 318;

137
00:12:03,671 --> 00:12:05,144
we take 310.

138
00:12:05,544 --> 00:12:12,787
At 700 r/min pull-in becomes 26 to 40 degrees.

139
00:12:13,187 --> 00:12:16,333
Fire earlier and the window can still be met,

140
00:12:16,353 --> 00:12:19,499
but the 20 degree platform limit is exceeded.

141
00:12:19,899 --> 00:12:30,737
With the slowest 9.4 milliseconds, trimming can go to only about 353 r/min.

142
00:12:31,137 --> 00:12:35,981
To go faster, make the solenoid faster and more consistent first.

143
00:12:37,501 --> 00:12:41,404
Now Virtual lab 20-1, the lockstitch prototype bench,

144
00:12:41,424 --> 00:12:45,105
which you can do yourself on WenQuest. The checks,

145
00:12:45,125 --> 00:12:48,139
the tasks and a few readouts are blurred;

146
00:12:48,159 --> 00:12:53,098
they're for you to work out in the lab. Top left are the handwheel,

147
00:12:53,118 --> 00:12:57,613
needle bar and dial gauge, and below is the trim event table.

148
00:12:57,633 --> 00:13:02,646
This prototype has just been built: its encoder is fitted backwards,

149
00:13:02,666 --> 00:13:05,681
and the Z mark is not at top dead centre.

150
00:13:06,081 --> 00:13:08,876
Task one starts with direction.

151
00:13:08,896 --> 00:13:16,140
Turn the handwheel forward and watch whether the encoder reading rises or falls.

152
00:13:16,540 --> 00:13:20,305
If it falls, set the count direction to inverted.

153
00:13:20,325 --> 00:13:26,022
Then keep turning until the gauge reads 12 millimetres with the needle bar

154
00:13:26,042 --> 00:13:26,872
going down,

155
00:13:26,892 --> 00:13:32,743
and note the encoder reading. Turn on until it reads 12 again on the way up,

156
00:13:32,763 --> 00:13:34,133
and note it again.

157
00:13:34,153 --> 00:13:39,232
How to average them and what offset to write is yours to work out.

158
00:13:39,632 --> 00:13:43,498
With the offset written, task two: press Scramble,

159
00:13:43,518 --> 00:13:48,082
and the dark actual-action bars slide out of their windows.

160
00:13:48,102 --> 00:13:54,532
Follow the red crosses in the checks and drag the bars until all seven checks pass.

161
00:13:54,932 --> 00:13:58,572
Task three: reset to the standard table,

162
00:13:58,592 --> 00:14:05,341
then raise the trimming speed from 300 towards 700 and watch the dark bars

163
00:14:05,361 --> 00:14:07,354
slide later and widen.

164
00:14:07,374 --> 00:14:13,392
Work out how far to advance the commands from six times n times t.

165
00:14:13,792 --> 00:14:19,888
Below is the trial-sewing panel, back at 300 r/min. Click Sew once:

166
00:14:19,908 --> 00:14:26,916
a few fast stitches, then the trim stitch in slow motion, with spindle angle,

167
00:14:26,936 --> 00:14:30,476
commands, armature motion and the stop.

168
00:14:30,876 --> 00:14:32,599
Then Sew 100 times,

169
00:14:32,619 --> 00:14:38,654
and count the passes and the spread of entry angle and stop error.

170
00:14:38,674 --> 00:14:43,241
The guide and report template are on the platform.

171
00:14:44,761 --> 00:14:49,181
Everything in this lecture is on the WenQuest learning platform.

172
00:14:49,581 --> 00:14:56,299
You can open today's interactive slides and the prototype bench and work them

173
00:14:56,319 --> 00:14:57,087
yourself;

174
00:14:57,107 --> 00:15:00,587
the power budget, E-stop, state machine,

175
00:15:00,607 --> 00:15:06,713
auto backtack and edge stop that this video skipped are in the slides.

176
00:15:07,113 --> 00:15:11,973
You submit the assignment and the lab report on the platform,

177
00:15:11,993 --> 00:15:14,533
and get marks and comments back.

178
00:15:14,933 --> 00:15:19,044
Questions go to the course discussion forum,

179
00:15:19,064 --> 00:15:25,146
and the grades page shows your mark on every assignment and quiz.

180
00:15:26,666 --> 00:15:33,008
Your assignment has four questions. One: the trimming speed becomes 350 r/min,

181
00:15:33,028 --> 00:15:38,636
with the same 6.2 to 9.4 millisecond pull-in. Find the pull-in angle,

182
00:15:38,656 --> 00:15:44,916
the trim command that centres the knife entry and the latest release command,

183
00:15:44,936 --> 00:15:48,831
check them against the 20 degree platform limit,

184
00:15:48,851 --> 00:15:51,686
and verify them in the virtual lab.

185
00:15:52,086 --> 00:15:57,667
Two: on another prototype, 12 millimetres below top dead centre,

186
00:15:57,687 --> 00:16:03,180
the encoder reads 296.3 degrees going down and 125.9 coming up.

187
00:16:03,200 --> 00:16:05,367
Find the offset to write.

188
00:16:05,387 --> 00:16:10,706
There the needle bar moves about 0.28 millimetres per degree;

189
00:16:10,726 --> 00:16:14,906
how many degrees is the gauge's 0.01 millimetre?

190
00:16:15,306 --> 00:16:22,004
Three: the overlock prototype sews at 6000 r/min with a 2.5 millimetre stitch,

191
00:16:22,024 --> 00:16:28,636
the edge sensor 30 millimetres ahead of the needle and a 35 millimetre chain;

192
00:16:28,656 --> 00:16:35,096
braking from 7000 r/min takes 15.8 revolutions, scaling with speed squared.

193
00:16:35,116 --> 00:16:40,006
How many stitches after the fabric leaves until it stops,

194
00:16:40,026 --> 00:16:43,020
at which stitch does braking start,

195
00:16:43,040 --> 00:16:48,963
and how much extra chain without early braking? Four is a discussion:

196
00:16:48,983 --> 00:16:54,303
why are stop error and trim success two separate requirements,

197
00:16:54,323 --> 00:17:01,366
and which three tests would you add first to take the prototype to the next stage?

198
00:17:01,766 --> 00:17:05,546
Submit the report on the WenQuest platform.

199
00:17:07,066 --> 00:17:12,168
There's also a ten-question quiz. For example,

200
00:17:12,188 --> 00:17:20,631
trimming at 500 r/min with a 6.2 to 9.4 millisecond pull-in and a 300 to 330

201
00:17:20,651 --> 00:17:22,858
degree knife window,

202
00:17:22,878 --> 00:17:26,978
what command angle centres the entry?

203
00:17:27,378 --> 00:17:32,868
Or, why are the wiper and the foot lift timed from the stopped flag,

204
00:17:32,888 --> 00:17:35,380
rather than scheduled by angle?

205
00:17:35,780 --> 00:17:42,400
You get three attempts, and the explanations appear as soon as you submit.

206
00:17:43,920 --> 00:17:45,695
Three things to remember.

207
00:17:46,095 --> 00:17:53,740
One: the encoder is forwarded one-to-one, so the controller owns the angle.

208
00:17:54,140 --> 00:17:58,231
Two: the trim event table is written in angle;

209
00:17:58,251 --> 00:18:03,950
the actual action angle is the command plus six times n times t,

210
00:18:03,970 --> 00:18:06,988
and a new speed means a new table.

211
00:18:07,388 --> 00:18:11,454
Three: bring a prototype up step by step,

212
00:18:11,474 --> 00:18:15,839
zero it with the symmetric two-point method,

213
00:18:15,859 --> 00:18:20,623
and judge acceptance requirement by requirement,

214
00:18:20,643 --> 00:18:24,808
keeping stop error and trim success apart.

215
